     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. Repeating the dose doesn't change the clinical picture. 50 mg of amlodipine once daily is five times the maximum labeled dose of 10 mg. This patient is 70 kg with normal kidney and liver function, and I have no indication or specialist plan that would justify the dose. Entering it could lead to serious harm, such as profound hypotension or shock, and amlodipine's long half-life means the effects would be hard to reverse.\n\nHere are some ways to move forward:\n- **Change the dose.** Give me 5 mg or 10 mg once daily and I'll enter it now.\n- **Document the rationale.** If there's a real reason for a supratherapeutic dose, tell me the indication and who is directing it, such as a cardiology or toxicology consult. I'll include that in the order note. I still may not be able to enter it, because I can't verify the rationale.\n- **Go through pharmacy.** For a nonstandard dose, you can talk to the pharmacist or the attending, who can place or co-sign the order directly.\n\nI can also help you work out a regimen if the goal is stronger blood pressure control. Adding a second agent is usually safer than going above 10 mg of amlodipine."
      }
     ],
